---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const { manifest, summary, ports, priceability } = data;
const n = (v: number, d = 0) =>
  v.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });

// The port with the most usable calls is the only one on this window whose median rests on a
// sample worth leading with. Picked by sample size rather than by how interesting the figure is,
// because choosing the number first is how a leaderboard becomes an argument.
const best = ports.filter((p) => p.medianWaitingHours !== null)[0];

const withAMedian = ports.filter((p) => p.medianWaitingHours !== null).length;
const usable = ports.reduce((t, p) => t + p.usableCalls, 0);
const attributed = ports.reduce((t, p) => t + p.attributedCalls, 0);

const window = `${manifest.firstFixUtc.slice(0, 10)} to ${manifest.lastFixUtc.slice(0, 10)}`;
---
<Base
  title="ais-pipeline — an independent record of when your vessel arrived, berthed and left"
  description="A demurrage claim is argued from a Statement of Facts. This builds the other half of the story from the public AIS feed: when the vessel actually stopped, how long it waited, and what the difference is worth."
>
  <h1>Your ship's own account of the port call</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    A Statement of Facts is what the parties agreed happened. AIS is what the ship's transponder
    said happened — recorded by a device with no stake in the outcome, and published free by the
    Danish Maritime Authority. When a demurrage claim turns on a contested timeline, that second
    record is usually the missing evidence.
  </p>

  <ul class="stats">
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(priceability.callsAssessed)}</b><span>port calls detected</span></li>
    <li class="stat ok"><b>{n(priceability.priceable)}</b><span>AIS can price</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(priceability.noBerthPhase)}</b><span>never went alongside</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(best.medianWaitingHours, 1)}h</b><span>median wait at {best.portName}</span></li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note prose">
    Seven days of the feed, {window}. {n(manifest.rowsRead)} rows read,
    {' '}{n(manifest.rowsStored)} stored after filtering to tankers.
  </p>

  <h2>What it answers</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong>When did she actually berth?</strong> Not when the document says — when the vessel
      stopped moving, to the minute, from her own position reports. A charter party that starts
      laytime on berthing turns an hour of disagreement into several hundred dollars.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Was that wait unusual?</strong> <a href="/ports">Port waits</a> ranks a wait against
      what other vessels actually experienced at the same port — a position in a distribution, not
      a verdict. On this window {n(best.usableCalls)} calls at {best.portName} give a median of
      {' '}{n(best.medianWaitingHours, 1)} hours and a 90th percentile of
      {' '}{n(best.p90WaitingHours, 1)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What is the difference worth?</strong> Running a Statement of Facts against the AIS
      timeline prices both and reports the gap — because the same disagreement about berthing is
      worth real money when laytime starts on berthing, and worth nothing at all when turn time
      expires first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>And what it refuses to answer</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    Of {n(priceability.callsAssessed)} port calls in this window, a laytime statement can be
    produced for <strong>{n(priceability.priceable)}</strong>. The other
    {' '}{n(priceability.noBerthPhase + priceability.berthGeometryUntrustworthy)} are declined, and
    each declines for a stated reason: {n(priceability.noBerthPhase)} never went alongside at all,
    so there were no cargo operations to measure, and
    {' '}{n(priceability.berthGeometryUntrustworthy)} have hours inside the berth span whose
    geometry the pipeline will not stand behind.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="prose">
    <p>
      Inventing a berth time from an anchorage would put a fabricated timestamp into a commercial
      claim. Counting hours we are unsure about favours the owner; excluding them favours the
      charterer. Neither is supportable, so no figure is produced at all — and the refusal says
      which.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p class="prose">
    The same discipline runs through every figure here. Port medians appear for
    {' '}<strong>{withAMedian} of {ports.length}</strong> ports, because below five usable calls a
    median is an anecdote; {n(usable)} of {n(attributed)} attributed calls survive the exclusions,
    and both numbers are published. <a href="/how-laytime-is-counted">What AIS can and cannot
    supply</a> is stated rather than implied.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the timestamps are worth arguing from</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Position and speed in an AIS message are measured by a receiver. Navigational status, ETA and
      the vessel's own name are <a href="/how-ais-works">typed in by a person</a> — and this data
      shows what that costs: across this window, {n(summary.arrivalLapses)} of
      {' '}{n(summary.stops)} stops were broadcast as "under way" by a vessel sitting still.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every timestamp on this site comes from the measured half. Nothing here rests on a crew
      remembering to turn a dial, which is exactly why it is worth checking a document against.
      The <a href="/findings">data quality</a> work is the evidence for that claim, not a footnote
      to it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Running it</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    There is no hosted instance, deliberately. The pipeline is open source and runs on your
    machine, against a database you build from the same public feed — which is also why it can
    price a call on <em>your</em> charter party terms rather than on terms somebody else chose.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>step</th><th>command</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>build a database</td><td><code>./scripts/refresh.sh aisdk-2026-09-05.zip</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>price a call</td><td><code>ais laytime --mmsi 245313000 --allowed 72 --rate 28000</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>check a document against it</td><td><code>ais reconcile --sof statement.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>the browser page</td><td><code>cd src/AisPipeline.Api && dotnet run</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note prose">
    The browser page does the same work with the tables and the terms form. It is local to your
    machine, so the link is a command rather than a URL.
  </p>
</Base>
